@@ -967,6 +967,23 @@ to all the metrics scraped by the given `vmagent` instance:
 
 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+### Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+Sometimes a misconfigured service discovery source (a too broad cloud API query, a catalog query without proper filters, etc.)
+may suddenly return an abnormally big number of targets for a single scrape job (`job_name`). Scraping all of these targets at once
+may spike CPU and memory usage of `vmagent` and degrade scraping of other jobs running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+In order to protect against this, the maximum number of active scrape targets a single job can have at once can be limited
+via the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag (applies to all the jobs) or via the `max_scrape_targets` option
+in the [`scrape_config`](#how-to-collect-metrics-in-prometheus-format) section of an individual job (overrides `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` for that job).
+This limit applies to the total number of targets discovered for the job, regardless of which service discovery mechanism(s) produced them.
+By default, this limit isn't set, e.g. it is unlimited.
+
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds the configured limit, then only the first `N` targets are scraped,
+while the rest are excluded from scraping. Excluded targets are shown at the `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page
+with the `DROPPED (job target limit exceeded)` status, so it is easy to notice which job(s) are currently hitting the limit
+and how many targets are excluded because of it.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
